{"event":"step_update","step_update":{"conversation_id":"ba80eee0-2d97-4e5a-aeec-6d1a103bdea7","step_index":9,"state":"DONE","step_type":"tool","tool_name":"run_command","duration_seconds":1.8833609999999998,"tool_info":{"name":"run_command","parameters":{"CommandLine":"ls -la /tmp/h-L6idUi/notes \u0026\u0026 head -n 50 /tmp/h-L6idUi/notes/* 2\u003e/dev/null || true"},"output":"\u003ctruncated 78 lines\u003e\n\r\n- **Has the problem:** at least one check comes back FAIL.\r\n- **Doesn't have it:** every check is PASS, or a deliberate decision you can name (for example \"tools/list needs a token on purpose, because …\"). NOT CHECKED rows (for example logging, when nobody gave the agent the source or the logs) don't count either way.\r\n- **Incomplete — record nothing:** if C2–C5 could not be run (the tools need a token you did not give), no FAIL does not mean \"doesn't have it\". Say so in the report and do not record a result.\r\n\r\n## Reproduce this\r\n\r\nThis contract does not fix anything. It produces a report: one row per check, PASS / FAIL / DECIDED / NOT CHECKED, the evidence, and the worklore story that fixes each failure. The fixing is a separate decision, one story at a time.\r\n\r\nPrerequisites: the server's MCP URL; `curl` or Python 3 (standard library only). Optional, each unlocks a check: a bearer token the server accepts, read access to the server's source, its name in the official MCP Registry.\r\n\r\nYour agent will need from you: the URL; whether it may use a token (and where it is — read it from an environment variable, never print it); which tools are safe to call if the server does not mark them `readOnlyHint: true`; one argument value that should make a read-only tool refuse (an id that does not exist); where the source is, if you want the logging check.\r\n\r\nGround rules — the agent must follow all of them:\r\n- READ-ONLY. Send only `initialize`, `notifications/initialized`, `ping`, `server/discover`, `tools/list`, `resources/list`, `prompts/list`, and `tools/call` on tools whose `annotations.readOnlyHint` is `true` (or that the human named as safe). Never call a tool that writes, deletes, publishes, pays or sends — not even with \"fake\" arguments. Do not edit any file.\r\n- A handful of requests, one at a time. No load, no fuzzing.\r\n- Never print a token. Put it in a header from an environment variable.\r\n- Report what you saw, with the exact status codes and short excerpts. If a check cannot be run, write NOT CHECKED and why; never guess a PASS.\r\n\r\nTalking to the server: POST JSON-RPC to the URL with `Content-Type: application/json` and `Accept: application/json, text/event-stream`. Some servers answer as `text/event-stream`; then the JSON is on the `data:` line. If the response carries an `Mcp-Session-Id` header, send it back on every later request — that is \"the same session\". Start every session with `initialize` (`{\"protocolVersion\":\"2025-06-18\",\"capabilities\":{},\"clientInfo\":{\"name\":\"checkup\",\"version\":\"1\"}}`), then `notifications/initialized`.\r\n\r\nSteps — the checks:\r\n\r\n1. **C1 · The tool list answers without a token.** With NO `Authorization` header: `initialize`, then `tools/list`. PASS: both answer 200 with a result, and the tools are there (count them). FAIL: 401/403 — no directory, scanner or stranger can see what the server offers, and nobody outside your repository can test it. DECIDED if the human says it is closed on purpose and can say why. Fix: [My MCP server hid its own tool list behind a login](https://worklore.dev/s/2026-09-27-my-mcp-server-hid-its-own-tool-list-behind-a-login). If it is closed and you have a token, continue the remaining checks with the token; if you have none, every check below that needs `tools/list` or `tools/call` is NOT CHECKED. Also send `server/discover` with no token. PASS: 200 with a result or a `-32601` error. FAIL: 401 — Claude Code 2.1.299+ probes with it before `initialize` and marks the whole server needs-auth on a 401, so anonymous users get no tools even though `initialize` and `tools/list` are open.\r\n\r\n2. **C2 · A tool refusal comes back as a result, not a protocol error.** Call a read-only tool with an id that does not exist, and separately with a required argument missing. PASS: HTTP 200 with a `result` that has `isError: true` and a readable message. FAIL: a JSON-RPC `error` (typically `-32602`) — a client SDK throws on that, so the model never reads \"not found\" and cannot correct itself; the 2025-11-25 spec revision moved input validation into tool results ([SEP-1303](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/community/seps/1303-input-validation-errors-as-tool-execution-errors)). An UNKNOWN tool name may stay a JSON-RPC error. Fix: [My live MCP test asserted the bug: a real client threw where my test passed](https://worklore.dev/s/2026-10-02-my-live-mcp-test-asserted-the-bug-a-real-client-threw-where-).\r\n\r\n3. **C3 · The session survives an error.** In the same session as C2 (same `Mcp-Session-Id`, if the server issued one), right after the refusal, make a normal call to a read-only tool with valid arguments. PASS: a normal result. FAIL: an error, a hang, or the session is gone. Fix: the same story as C2.\r\n\r\n4. **C4 · The published outputSchema is honoured.** Keep the schemas from the `tools/list` response — not from the source code. For every read-only tool that declares an `outputSchema`, make one call with valid arguments. PASS: no `isError`, `structuredContent` is present, and it validates against that schema (types, `required` fields, nested objects and arrays). FAIL: `isError` on a valid call, a missing `structuredContent`, or a mismatch — clients that validate results will reject them. An error result proves nothing about the schema, so it is a FAIL here, not a pass. Fix: [My tests passed because they agreed with me](https://worklore.dev/s/2026-09-28-my-tests-passed-because-they-agreed-with-me).\r\n\r\n\r\n==\u003e /tmp/h-L6idUi/notes/collecting-leads-with-apps-script.md \u003c==\r\n---\r\n\r\n# Collecting leads from a static site with Apps Script and a honeypot\r\n\r\nI keep a small site for our family kennel. It is a static page: no server, no\r\ndatabase, no build step.\r\n\r\nThis year's litter sold out in August and the next one is a year or more away,\r\nso the site had nothing left to sell while people kept arriving from search and\r\nfrom Instagram and asking anyway. I wanted a waiting list.\r\n\r\nThe reflex at that point is to reach for infrastructure. An AWS account with a\r\nLambda and a table behind it. A Supabase project. A form service with a monthly\r\nbill. I have used all three, and each one is a thing you then own: credentials\r\nto rotate, a free tier that quietly changes, a dashboard to log into a year\r\nlater when you have forgotten how you wired it. For a kennel that produces a\r\nlitter every two years, that is not engineering. It is a subscription to future\r\nconfusion.\r\n\r\nThe answer was a spreadsheet, and I already had one. Google Apps Script turns\r\nit into an endpoint: Extensions, Apps Script, about twenty lines with a\r\n`doPost`, deploy it as a web app, and the sheet accepts POSTs, appends rows and\r\nemails me. Nothing to create, nothing to pay, no credentials in the page. The\r\ndata and the endpoint live in the same place, which is what I actually care\r\nabout in eighteen months when someone from that list writes back.\r\n\r\nTwo practical notes. A hidden field catches bots without a captcha. And the\r\nbrowser will not POST JSON to Apps Script directly, because it never answers the\r\nCORS preflight; sending it as `text/plain` with `no-cors` makes it a simple\r\nrequest and it goes through, at the cost of an opaque response. The sheet and\r\nthe email are the confirmation, not the fetch.\r\n\r\nSimple things should stay simple.\r\n\r\n## Reproduce this\r\n\r\nPrerequisites: a static site you can edit and deploy, and a Google account.\r\n\r\nYour agent will need from you: which spreadsheet to write into (or permission\r\nto create one), the address to notify, and the list of fields to collect.\r\n\r\nSteps:\r\n\r\n1. Create a Google Sheet for submissions. Its ID is the long string in the URL\r\n   between `/d/` and `/edit`.\r\n2. In that sheet choose Extensions, then Apps Script. Write a `doPost(e)` that\r\n   parses `e.postData.contents` as JSON; returns success immediately and writes\r\n   nothing if the honeypot field is non-empty; rejects missing required fields;\r\n   takes a `LockService.getScriptLock()` before writing so two submissions in\r\n   the same second cannot interleave; appends a row via\r\n"}}}
